{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"1700e069f0e30e29","type":"text","text":"# How Liquid is the Company?\n\n## Current Ratio\n\n* **Current Ratio = Current Assets/Current Liabilities**\n* Can the company cover outstanding debt with its current assets.\n* Greater than 1 is fine, although sometimes 2 is used as some current assets not easily turned into cash.\n\n## Quick Ratio\n\n* **Quick Ratio = (Current Assets - Inventories)/Current Liabilities** \n* considered better as inventories can be less liquid\n\nHigh not necessarily better as could indicate holding too much cash and not investing in long term assets.\n\n\n","x":-500,"y":-1200,"width":620,"height":540},
		{"id":"460741ae9efefd92","type":"text","text":"# Is the company generating enough returns from its assets\n\n### Net profit margin\n\n* Net profit margin = Net income/revenues.\n* Measures percentage of revenues that is profit.\n* So looking below profit margin would be 76/650 = 0.1169 = 11.69%\n\n![[Pasted image 20260912093933.png]]\n\n### Return on assets/ROA\n\n* How much return as measured by net income is generated per monetary unit invested in total assets.\n* Return on assets = Net income/Total assets\n\n## Basic earning power\n\n* Basic earning power = Operating income/Total assets\n* this excludes financing (interest expenses and tax)\n\n\n## Operating Profit Margin\n\nOperating profit margin = Operating income/revenues\n\n* Measures percentage of revenues that is operating profit\n\n## Asset Turnover\n\nRevenues/Total Assets\n\n* How effectively a company uses total assets to generate sales volume\n\n","x":-620,"y":-260,"width":860,"height":751},
		{"id":"fa641035c4bf31d2","type":"text","text":"# Using Financial Ratios\n\nProvides context for comparing periods (time series) and companies (cross sectional). Can help compare companies of different sizes.\n\n1. How liquid is the company\n2. is the company generating enough return from its assets\n3. Does the company have too much debt\n4. Is the company providing sufficient return for its shareholders\n\n","x":360,"y":-660,"width":940,"height":300},
		{"id":"1a2eb17924e4a667","x":240,"y":-1330,"width":960,"height":400,"type":"text","text":"# Does the Company have too much debt?\n\n## Debt to equity ratio\n\nDebt to equity ratio = Debt/Equity\n\n* Only counts interest bearing debt (not accounts payable, accrued expenses)\n* Measures how much debt the company has relative to equity.\n\n## Equity multiplier ratio\n\nFinancial leverage = Equity multiplier = Total assets/equity\n\nmeasures amount of total assets supported by one monetary unit of equity. The greater the equity multiplier the more debt is being used to finance assets.\n"}
	],
	"edges":[
